[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Queues: FIFO Principle and Implementation"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to Queues"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"A queue is a fundamental data structure that follows the First-In, First-Out (FIFO) principle. This means that the first element added to the queue will be the first element removed. Think of it like a line at a store – the first person in line is the first person served."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"FIFO Principle"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"The core concept of a queue is FIFO. Elements are added to the rear (enqueue operation) and removed from the front (dequeue operation). This ensures fairness and order in processing elements."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Queue Operations"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Enqueue: Adds an element to the rear of the queue."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Dequeue: Removes the element from the front of the queue."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Peek/Front: Returns the element at the front of the queue without removing it."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"IsEmpty: Checks if the queue is empty."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"IsFull: Checks if the queue is full (relevant for array-based implementations)."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementation using Arrays"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Arrays provide a simple way to implement queues. However, a naive implementation can be inefficient due to shifting elements during dequeue operations. A circular array implementation is often preferred."},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In a circular array, the 'front' and 'rear' indices wrap around to the beginning of the array when they reach the end. This avoids shifting elements and improves performance."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"// Array-based Queue Implementation (Circular)
class Queue {
  constructor(capacity) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.queue = new Array(capacity);
    this.front = 0;
    this.rear = -1;
    this.size = 0;
  }

  enqueue(element) {
    if (this.isFull()) {
      return 'Queue is full';
    }
    this.rear = (this.rear + 1) % this.capacity;
    this.queue[this.rear] = element;
    this.size++;
  }

  dequeue() {
    if (this.isEmpty()) {
      return 'Queue is empty';
    }
    const element = this.queue[this.front];
    this.front = (this.front + 1) % this.capacity;
    this.size--;
    return element;
  }

  peek() {
    if (this.isEmpty()) {
      return 'Queue is empty';
    }
    return this.queue[this.front];
  }

  isEmpty() {
    return this.size === 0;
  }

  isFull() {
    return this.size === this.capacity;
  }
}

// Example usage:
const queue = new Queue(5);
queue.enqueue(10);
queue.enqueue(20);
console.log(queue.dequeue()); // Output: 10
console.log(queue.peek());    // Output: 20"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementation using Linked Lists"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Linked lists offer a dynamic way to implement queues. Unlike arrays, linked lists can grow or shrink as needed, avoiding the fixed-size limitation. Enqueue operations add nodes to the tail, and dequeue operations remove nodes from the head."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"// Linked List-based Queue Implementation
class Node {
  constructor(data) {
    this.data = data;
    this.next = null;
  }
}

class Queue {
  constructor() {
    this.front = null;
    this.rear = null;
    this.size = 0;
  }

  enqueue(data) {
    const newNode = new Node(data);
    if (this.isEmpty()) {
      this.front = newNode;
      this.rear = newNode;
    } else {
      this.rear.next = newNode;
      this.rear = newNode;
    }
    this.size++;
  }

  dequeue() {
    if (this.isEmpty()) {
      return 'Queue is empty';
    }
    const data = this.front.data;
    this.front = this.front.next;
    if (this.front === null) {
      this.rear = null; // Queue is now empty
    }
    this.size--;
    return data;
  }

  peek() {
    if (this.isEmpty()) {
      return 'Queue is empty';
    }
    return this.front.data;
  }

  isEmpty() {
    return this.size === 0;
  }
}

// Example usage:
const queue = new Queue();
queue.enqueue(10);
queue.enqueue(20);
console.log(queue.dequeue()); // Output: 10
console.log(queue.peek());    // Output: 20"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Comparison of Implementations"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Arrays: Simple to implement, but fixed size (unless using dynamic arrays). Circular arrays improve efficiency by avoiding shifting."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Linked Lists: Dynamic size, more flexible. Requires more memory due to node pointers."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"Queues are essential data structures with numerous applications. Understanding the FIFO principle and the trade-offs between array and linked list implementations is crucial for efficient software development."}]
